\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs32\b newVM Performance Benchmark\b0\fs22\par
\fs24\b MatMult / LinearSolve / Invert / FFT-IFFT / EigDecompose, all four TVMobj* types\b0\par
\b Library-backed (MKL + IPP + OpenBLAS + FFTW3) vs PUREPASCAL, Windows 11 / x86_64, -O3 -Sa-\b0\par
\fs22\par

\b Machine:\b0  AMD Ryzen 9 7900 12-Core Processor, 24 logical CPUs, 31.1 GiB RAM\par
\b OS:\b0  Windows 11 Home, build 10.0.26200\par
\b Compiler flags:\b0  fpc -O3 -Sa- (optimisation level 3, assertions/range checks explicitly disabled - IncludeAssertionCode is off for this standalone plain-fpc build, unlike newVMtest.lpi's .lpi-driven build)\par
\b Backends detected by ./newvmconfigure:\b0  MKL (mkl_rt.3.dll), IPP (ippcore+ippvm+ipps), OpenBLAS (openblas.dll), FFTW3 (double+single) - all found; Apple Accelerate not found (Darwin-only, expected on Windows). The "real" build for this machine is fully library-backed (PUREPASCAL and PUREPASCAL_BLAS both inactive, HAVE_FFTW active). The PUREPASCAL comparison column below was produced by hand-forcing newVMConfig.inc to {\f1 \{$DEFINE PUREPASCAL\}}\f0 /{\f1 \{$DEFINE PUREPASCAL_BLAS\}}\f0  and commenting out {\f1 \{$DEFINE HAVE_FFTW\}}\f0  (same technique as perf/x64linuxperformance.txt / perf/OSXperformance.txt, extended here to also force FFT/IFFT onto their PurePascal direct-DFT fallback so that section is a genuine library-vs-PurePascal comparison too), then restoring the real per-machine config from a backup afterwards - newvmconfigure was not re-run, so the restored file is byte-identical to what it was before this benchmark run.\par
\fs22\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs24\b Test suite verification:\b0  ./newVMtest (full 262-test FPCUnit suite, all five TVMobj*/TVMobjI types) - 262 run, 0 errors, 0 failures, against the real (library-backed) build, re-verified again after restoring the real config post-benchmark.\par
\fs22\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs24\b\i WINDOWS-SPECIFIC FINDING: newVMbench.lpr still crashes as shipped on this platform (reconfirmed this run)\i0\b0\par
\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22
Running ./newVMbench unmodified aborts partway through the single-precision complex (newVMComplexSingle.pas) section at N=100, with the same EInvalidOp ("Invalid floating point operation") this platform's earlier benchmark run (see the previous windows11ryzen9performance.txt, now superseded by this file) already isolated to PurePascalLUC's Gaussian elimination hitting a transient denormal/borderline value that Linux/macOS silently flush under their default FPU exception masks but that FPC's Windows runtime leaves unmasked. This reproduced identically in THIS run's library-backed build too (InvertC/LinearSolveC always run PurePascalLUC's kernel on every platform, library-backed or not - see the per-operation backend banner each table is printed under), confirming this is not something the earlier -O3 rebuild happened to dodge.\par
The timings below were captured from a locally-patched, uncommitted copy of newVMbench.lpr with {\f1 Math.SetExceptionMask([exInvalidOp, exOverflow, exZeroDivide, exUnderflow, exPrecision, exDenormalized])}\f0  called at the very top of the main block, guarded by {\f1 \{$IFDEF WINDOWS\}}\f0  - the same mask, in the same place, as the prior run used. newVMbench.lpr itself was reverted to its committed state (git diff clean) immediately after both benchmark runs completed; this remains an upstream item worth fixing properly (either adding the mask permanently, guarded to Windows, or narrowing it to just around PurePascalLUC's elimination step) rather than something fixed as part of this reporting task. Every other row, in every other type/operation/N combination, completed identically with or without the mask.\par
\fs22\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22\b Notes:\b0\par
Residuals are Frobenius-norm ||A*x-b|| (solve) / ||A*Ainv-I|| (invert) / round-trip ||IFFT(FFT(A))-A|| (FFT) / ||A*v-lambda*v|| worst-case over all eigenpairs (EigDecompose); both builds compute the same correct answer to within precision-appropriate tolerance in every case. Speedup = PUREPASCAL time / library-backed time (a value below 1.00x means the library-backed call was actually SLOWER at that N - this happens for several small/medium-N cells below, see the summary). All timings in milliseconds, single run per row (not averaged).\par
N=10 anomalies: several "first LAPACKE/FFTW call of the process" cells are wildly out of line with their neighbours - LinearSolve(real double) N=10 costs 5.43ms library-backed vs a sub-resolution 0.00ms PUREPASCAL (lapacke_dgesv is the first LAPACKE_* call the whole process makes, MatMult's cblas_dgemm having already run and paid no such cost); FFT(double) N=10 costs 0.74ms library-backed vs 0.01ms PUREPASCAL for the same reason (FFTW's fftw_plan_dft_1d pays a one-time plan-creation cost on its first call, independent of and analogous to MKL's lazy init); EigDecompose(double) N=10 costs 10.85ms library-backed vs 0.03ms PUREPASCAL, a similar first-call LAPACKE_dgeev effect - notably NOT explained by general MKL warm-up, since by the time RunBenchmarksEig runs, lapacke_dgesv/dgetrf/dgetri have already been called dozens of times earlier in the same process; dgeev's own internal workspace setup appears to carry its own separate one-time cost. These n/a-marked cells are excluded from the speedup column as meaningless rather than reported as a literal (near-infinite) multiplier.\par
\fs22\par

