\fs22\par

\pard\page

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs26\b Summary\b0\fs22\par
\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22
As expected, the gap between library-backed and PUREPASCAL grows sharply with N for every O(N^3) operation. MatMult sees the largest speedups among the linear-algebra trio (127x-353x at N=1000, MKL/OpenBLAS's cblas_?gemm vs a plain triple loop). LinearSolve is again the least favourable to the library at small/medium N - the real-type LinearSolve cells at N=100 actually run FASTER in PUREPASCAL than library-backed (0.16x and 0.68x "speedup", i.e. lapacke_?gesv's own call overhead outweighs a reasonably tight single-threaded LU at that size) - before the library pulls ahead again at N=1000 (34x-63x). Invert lands in between (56x-106x at N=1000), tracking LinearSolve's LU cost plus an extra O(N^3) triangular-solve pass. This is the same qualitative shape as the earlier windows11ryzen9performance.txt run (now superseded by this file), though the absolute speedup numbers differ somewhat from that run - unsurprising, since that run's exact build flags for newVMbench itself were not otherwise recorded, and this run additionally has the FFT/EigDecompose sections neither earlier platform log had at all.\par
FFT/IFFT show by far the largest speedups of anything in this report (up to 683x for single-precision FFT at N=1000) - expected, since this comparison compounds two effects at once: FFTW's O(N log N) algorithm against PPDirectDFT's/PPDirectDFTS's O(N^2) direct summation, on top of the usual hand-tuned-library-vs-plain-Pascal constant-factor gap the other operations show. EigDecompose shows the most modest gains of any operation tested (1.4x-2.1x at N=50/100, double), and even a case where PUREPASCAL wins outright (single precision, N=50, 0.29x) - LAPACKE's dgeev/sgeev carry comparatively high fixed overhead relative to the ported EISPACK-style Balance/ElmHes/Eltran/Hqr2/BalBak pipeline at these matrix sizes; the library's advantage would be expected to grow at larger N (untested here - see the N=1000 exclusion rationale above), consistent with every other operation's trend in this report.\par
Residuals confirm both variants compute the same correct answer at each N, precision, and operation (double ~1e-15 to 1e-11 for the LU-based trio, ~1e-15 to 1e-13 for EigDecompose, ~1e-15 to 1e-11 for FFT round-trip; single correspondingly looser at ~1e-6 to ~5e-2), consistent with the other two platforms' logs for the operations they share.\par
Two open items from this run worth flagging rather than glossing over: (1) the Windows-only EInvalidOp crash in PurePascalLUC at N>=100 reproduced again and needed the same uncommitted FPU-exception-mask workaround as before - still not fixed upstream in newVMbench.lpr itself; (2) LinearSolveZ/LinearSolveC's N=1000 timings differ sharply between the two builds (14.14ms vs 852.82ms; 7.72ms vs 3304.45ms) despite nominally running the exact same PurePascal-always kernel in both - not root-caused here, worth an isolated repro in a future pass.\par
